{"id":1335,"date":"2020-11-26T18:26:33","date_gmt":"2020-11-27T02:26:33","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1335"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:40:49","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:40:49","slug":"program-the-leds","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/program-the-leds\/","title":{"rendered":"Program the LEDs"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068c9c\" name=\"6a22162068c9c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Each LED is connected to a pin on the Smart Inventor Board, so the first thing you need to do is give each of your pins a variable name. These are going to go at the top of your code, so place your mouse cursor at void setup() and hit the enter key to give yourself room to do this. Then enter the code below.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int name = pin_number;\r\nint LEDPIN11 = 11;<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1336&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068dbe\" name=\"6a22162068dbe\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating more variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Just in case, keep line 1 as a comment so you remember how to create this particular variable.<\/p>\n<p>Create variables for pins 11 through 18. Unless you can type super fast, copy and paste is your friend! Your code should look like the one below.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int LEDPIN11 = 11;\r\nint LEDPIN12 = 12;\r\nint LEDPIN13 = 13;\r\nint LEDPIN14 = 14;\r\nint LEDPIN15 = 15;\r\nint LEDPIN16 = 16;\r\nint LEDPIN17 = 17;\r\nint LEDPIN18 = 18;<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068e32\" name=\"6a22162068e32\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Learning pinMode() function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]pinMode( ) usually looks like the example below.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">pinMode(name of declared pin, pin assigned as either INPUT or OUTPUT);<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>When a pin is declared as an input, this means that the pin takes in or reads information. Other parts of your robot, like sensors, can also be declared as an input.<\/p>\n<p>When a pin is declared as an output, this means that the pin performs an action, like flashing a light or making a noise.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068e87\" name=\"6a22162068e87\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Use pinMode() function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Are you ready to use pinMode()? Use it to assign LEDPIN11 through LEDPIN18 as outputs.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">pinMode(LEDPIN11,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN12,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN13,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN14,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN15,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN16,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN17,OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN18,OUTPUT);<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068ee6\" name=\"6a22162068ee6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>digitalWrite() function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]digitalWrite( ) usually looks like the example below.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">digitalWrite(name of declared pin, pin state\u00a0either HIGH\u00a0or LOW);<\/pre>\n<p>When an LED\u2019s state is high, the LED will turn on. When the LED\u2019s state is\u00a0low,\u00a0the LED will turn off.<\/p>\n<p>You can assign pin states to your LEDs in void loop() since they will be running continuously. Your code should look like the one below.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">digitalWrite(LEDPIN11, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN12, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN13, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN14, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN15, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN16, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN17, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN18, HIGH);\r\n\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN11, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN12, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN13, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN14, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN15, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN16, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN17, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN18, LOW);<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068f39\" name=\"6a22162068f39\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>delay() function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Quick review! delay( ) usually looks like the code below.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">delay( time in milliseconds\u00a0);<\/pre>\n<p>delay() is a function that will define how long an action should be performed. There are 1000 milliseconds in 1 second, so if your action should run for 3 seconds, your delay() time should be 3000.<\/p>\n<p>You can add one second delays to the end of each digital write block.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068f8d\" name=\"6a22162068f8d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your final code should look something like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">\/\/declares global variables used in the code\r\nint LEDPIN11 = 11;\r\nint LEDPIN12 = 12;\r\nint LEDPIN13 = 13;\r\nint LEDPIN14 = 14;\r\nint LEDPIN15 = 15;\r\nint LEDPIN16 = 16;\r\nint LEDPIN17 = 17;\r\nint LEDPIN18 = 18;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n\/\/pin assignments\r\npinMode(LEDPIN11, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN12, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN13, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN14, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN15, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN16, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN17, OUTPUT);\r\npinMode(LEDPIN18, OUTPUT);\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n\/\/sets the LED state to HIGH\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN11, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN12, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN13, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN14, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN15, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN16, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN17, HIGH);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN18, HIGH);\r\ndelay(1000);\r\n\r\n\/\/sets the LED state to LOW\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN11, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN12, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN13, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN14, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN15, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN16, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN17, LOW);\r\ndigitalWrite(LEDPIN18, LOW);\r\ndelay(1000);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>TIP: press ctrl + t to auto format your code.<\/p>\n<p>Try changing up your code so you can make an LED light show![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162068fd6\" name=\"6a22162068fd6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Video<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Still not sure about the LED program, or just want to make sure you did it right? Watch the video![\/vc_column_text][vc_video link=&#8221;https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/watch?v=ViJt3tWI0Wo&#8221; align=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Creating variables [vc_column_text]Each LED is connected to a pin on the Smart Inventor Board, so the first thing you need to do is give each of your&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/program-the-leds\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1736,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":802,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1760,1777,1808,1770,1793,423,626,1533,1438,853,144,59,171,63,209],"lesson-tag":[2163,2123,2216,2122,2171,2190,2191,2117],"class_list":["post-1335","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-leds","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-rokit-smart","lesson-tag-trailer","lesson-tag-trailer-bot","lesson-tag-variables","module-trailer","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1335","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":2,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1335\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1339,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1335\/revisions\/1339"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1736"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1335"}],"wp: